---
// The oracle as one picture: the same inputs go to the game's own functions and to the Rust port,
// side by side, and every bit of what comes out is compared. Styles: PipelineDiagram.astro.
---

<figure class="diagram">
  <div class="diagram__scroll" tabindex="0" role="group" aria-label="Diagram: how the oracle compares the game with the port">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 960 372" role="img" aria-labelledby="oracle-title oracle-desc">
      <title id="oracle-title">The oracle</title>
      <desc id="oracle-desc">
        The same inputs, step by step, go to two places: the real acs.exe, mapped into the test program and called
        by the addresses from its debug symbols, and rustyAC's Rust port of the same functions. The numbers both
        give back are compared bit by bit. Either all are equal, or the port is fixed and the comparison runs again.
      </desc>
      <defs>
        <marker id="oracle-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
          <path d="M1 1 9 5 1 9" class="d-head" />
        </marker>
      </defs>

      {/* inputs */}
      <rect x="16" y="126" width="196" height="120" rx="12" class="d-box" />
      <text x="34" y="156" class="d-mono">inputs</text>
      <text x="34" y="184" class="d-strong">The same for both</text>
      <text x="34" y="207" class="d-text">the driver's controls,</text>
      <text x="34" y="226" class="d-text">step by step</text>

      {/* the game */}
      <rect x="296" y="20" width="318" height="136" rx="12" class="d-box" />
      <text x="314" y="50" class="d-mono">acs.exe, the real game</text>
      <text x="314" y="78" class="d-strong">The game's own functions</text>
      <text x="314" y="102" class="d-text">mapped into the test program</text>
      <text x="314" y="121" class="d-text">and called by their address</text>
      <text x="314" y="140" class="d-text">from acs.pdb</text>

      {/* the port */}
      <rect x="296" y="216" width="318" height="136" rx="12" class="d-box" />
      <text x="314" y="246" class="d-mono">rustyAC, the port</text>
      <text x="314" y="274" class="d-strong">The same functions in Rust</text>
      <text x="314" y="298" class="d-text">rewritten one to one,</text>
      <text x="314" y="317" class="d-text">in the same order of operations</text>

      {/* compare */}
      <rect x="698" y="118" width="246" height="136" rx="12" class="d-box d-box--accent" />
      <text x="716" y="148" class="d-mono">compare</text>
      <text x="716" y="176" class="d-strong">Every bit of every number</text>
      <text x="716" y="200" class="d-text">all equal: done</text>
      <text x="716" y="219" class="d-text">one bit off: not done,</text>
      <text x="716" y="238" class="d-text">fix the port and run again</text>

      <path d="M212 166 H254 V88 H290" class="d-line" marker-end="url(#oracle-arrow)" />
      <path d="M212 206 H254 V284 H290" class="d-line" marker-end="url(#oracle-arrow)" />
      <path d="M614 88 H656 V166 H692" class="d-line" marker-end="url(#oracle-arrow)" />
      <path d="M614 284 H656 V206 H692" class="d-line" marker-end="url(#oracle-arrow)" />
      <text x="664" y="80" class="d-mono">numbers out</text>
      <text x="664" y="302" class="d-mono">numbers out</text>
    </svg>
  </div>
  <figcaption class="muted">
    The game is never started. Its code is only borrowed, one function at a time, to answer the same question the
    port is asked.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
